I think one of the biggest questions around agentic finance isn’t “Can AI make financial decisions?”
@ama_protocol

We already know agents can analyze data, react to market conditions and execute predefined strategies.  The harder question is: can we trust them with real capital?  2/7 Imagine an autonomous trading agent managing a strategy you’ve spent years developing. You probably don’t want the agent’s logic, trading edge, positions or sensitive inputs exposed every time it interacts with an external system.  That creates a pretty interesting infrastructure problem.  3/7 This is where privacy becomes more than just a nice feature.  For professional traders and institutions, the strategy itself can be valuable information. If every decision has to be made transparently on a public execution layer, you potentially end up trading transparency for competitive advantage.  4/7 What I find interesting about A madeus is the focus on putting agents inside Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) while still making their computation verifiable.  The goal isn’t simply:  “Trust the AI.” It’s closer to:  “Verify what the system actually executed.”
